\section{A loop path with checked exits}

Gal and colleagues' \emph{Trace-based Just-in-Time Type Specialization for Dynamic Languages} describes TraceMonkey, which starts in a bytecode interpreter and records operations along a hot path. A root trace specializes a loop path to the types it sees. When behavior changes, a guard exits to the interpreter or to a compiled branch trace. A frequently taken exit can therefore become the start of a new recording, rather than a reason to duplicate a whole function. Nested loops introduce a choice: unrolling an entire inner loop into the outer trace could produce a large, trip-count-specific artifact. TraceMonkey uses nested trace trees so an outer trace can call a compiled inner trace with a compatible entry state and reason about its expected exit.

Unlike a branch profile, a trace records the order and representation of data dependencies through several bytecodes. The optimizer can propagate constants and remove temporary allocations across the recorded path. It cannot delete an instruction whose result is needed at a side exit, nor can it suppose that an observed branch direction will always recur. Guard metadata is part of the trace's semantics, not merely a debugging aid.

\section{Record one iteration, recover at the failed bytecode}

In a toy language \texttt{Add} performs exact integer addition but traps on a Boolean operand. The interpreter runs the loop below from PC 10. The array is immutable, the index and length fit in a machine integer, and entry adapters establish the index representation and an unboxed small-integer accumulator. The recording sees only small-integer elements.

\begin{verbatim}
10: if i >= len(a): goto 15
11: x = a[i]
12: sum = Add(sum, x)
13: i = i + 1
14: goto 10
15: return sum

trace at PC 10:
  guard 0 <= i < len(a)     else resume(10, a, i, sum)
  x = load(a, i)
  guard is_small_int(x)    else resume(12, a, i, sum, x)
  s1, overflow = add_checked(sum, unbox(x))
  guard not overflow     else resume(12, a, i, sum, x)
  jump trace(a, i+1, s1)
\end{verbatim}

The trace-exit invariant relates each failure to the interpreter \emph{before} its named instruction with exactly the required live values. At PC 12 the snapshot boxes the old \texttt{sum}, not the possibly wrapped \texttt{s1}, and includes \texttt{x}; returning to PC 10 would require a different snapshot and re-execute the load. Test \texttt{[2, 4, true]} starting at index 0 and sum 0: the failing addition must trap at index 2 with sum 6, not at index 3 or with an already modified sum. Test the overflow exit by adding 1 to the maximal small integer; the generic path should produce a larger integer, not a trap. A bridge for a hot overflow exit must accept the exit's state and preserve the same arbitrary-precision semantics. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-256} develops the recovery machinery further.

\Cref{fig:visual-253} marks the state at the actual guard exit for the preceding \texttt{[2,4,true]} example, before either the sum or the index is advanced.

