\section{Trace the machine that interprets the loop}

In \emph{Tracing the Meta-Level: PyPy's Tracing JIT Compiler}, Bolz, Cuni, Fija{\l}kowski and Rigo trace operations of the interpreter itself. Without guidance, the host interpreter's dispatch loop looks like the hot loop; a trace that returns after each dispatch fails to expose the guest loop's dataflow. The implementer identifies a merge point of the interpreter and a guest backward edge where tracing can start. In RPython terminology the bytecode identity and guest PC can be \emph{green} loop-key values; the frame and numerical arguments are \emph{red} runtime state. In the example, the tracer sees known opcodes as it advances from PC 10 through PC 14. Unrolling dispatch and folding those constants exposes the type checks and loads; the trace closes when control returns to the same guest loop key.

Bolz and Tratt's \emph{The Impact of Meta-Tracing on VM Design and Implementation} explains why annotations and VM design still matter. Promoting a value specializes to the recorded value by inserting a guard. An elidable helper may be folded for known inputs under the documented dependency conditions; an object lookup is not elidable merely because it usually yields the same result. An explicit version token can turn a mutable lookup into a query tied to one version. Promoting every observed receiver instead would cause needless exits and code growth.

