\subsection{Give a Side Trace Its Own Entry Contract}

A repeatedly taken guard exit can recruit a specialized bridge,
but a guard must continue to work before a bridge is available
and for states the bridge does not accept. Consider a toy trace
of a loop whose body adds a tagged signed delta, then emits the
sum. The root path handles nonnegative deltas; a negative-delta
bridge is compiled only after observing that exit. Assume the
example's arithmetic fits its scalar range, and that no emission
has yet occurred for the current iteration when the guard fails.
The snapshot supplies the current accumulator and the original
tagged delta. A reference continuation handles any other tag and
reports a type error for Booleans.

\begin{sudocode}[Dispatch a guard exit to a compatible bridge]
\State $S\gets$ describe state before addition at the failed guard
\State $b\gets$ published bridge for this root and guard
\If{$b$ exists and its entry contract accepts $S$}
  \State $d\gets$ unbox delta from $S$
    \Comment{Bridge already checked tag and sign}
  \State $s\gets S.sum+d$
  \State emit $s$ and continue at the next iteration
\Else
  \State record an exit hit for $S$ \Comment{Count guard, not loop}
  \State resume reference addition from $S$
\EndIf
\end{sudocode}

Start at sum 10 and run deltas $(4,-3,-2,\mathsf{true})$.
Arrange publication of the negative bridge after the first
negative exit. The root emits 14, reference recovery emits 11,
and the bridge emits 9. The Boolean goes through reference
recovery and traps before an emission. Repeat with no bridge:
the same observations must appear, but the second negative
delta also uses reference recovery. Log which path ran; the
result alone cannot demonstrate that the bridge was entered.

The invariant is exactly one semantic continuation per exit,
with the prefix of observations already committed when the
guard failed. A bridge specialized for one exit cannot be
reused by PC alone: a different guard can deliver different
live values or representations at that same PC. Count bridge
bytes against a per-root budget.

\section{Choose a useful recording boundary}

Dossier Chapters~12 and~14 explore specialization in interpreters and sharing semantics among engines. A self-specializing add node rewrites local dispatch; a meta-trace can remove dispatch across several such nodes. Dossier Chapter~41 proposes varying the granularity further. Under the blueprint's separation of exploration from assurance, a prototype can cap trace length and bridges per root, interpret upon exhausting the cap, and compare against block versioning (Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-254}). Measure not only the time of a stable loop but also total bytes, exit frequency and completed work on a loop with irregular branches. A trace that wins for one observed path is not evidence that enlarging the compilation region will win for the workload.
